{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 10, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e4b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli ruled from 1155 to 1194 a significant figure in the Crusader states of the Levant. He was a member of the Raymond family a prominent noble lineage originating in southern France. His reign was marked by both military campaigns and diplomatic maneuvering aimed at consolidating Tripoli's power and navigating the complex political landscape of the Holy Land.\n\n*Early Life and Ascension* Raymond was born around 1110. He inherited the County of Tripoli upon the death of Raymond II in 1155. His early years were characterized by challenges including threats from Muslim forces and internal political rivalries. He quickly established himself as a capable ruler focusing on strengthening the county's defenses and fostering economic prosperity. He faced constant pressure from the Zengid dynasty rulers of Syria particularly Nur ad-Din and later Saladin. Raymond skillfully balanced alliances with other Crusader states like Antioch and Jerusalem seeking to maintain a strategic advantage.\n\nRaymond III’s reign saw significant military activity. He engaged in numerous battles against the Zengids often employing a strategy of defensive warfare and opportunistic raids. He also participated in Crusades including the Third Crusade though his direct involvement was limited. He focused on maintaining control over Tripoli's coastal territories and its vital trade routes.  He invested in building fortifications and maintaining a standing army crucial for defending against incursions from the surrounding regions. He understood the importance of a strong military to protect the county’s independence.\n\n*Diplomacy and Alliances* A key aspect of Raymond III's rule was his diplomatic skill. He cultivated relationships with various powers including the Byzantine Empire and the Papacy. He understood the need for alliances to counter the growing power of the Zengids. He frequently engaged in negotiations seeking to secure military assistance or political support. He was known for his pragmatism and willingness to compromise when necessary.  His diplomatic efforts helped to maintain a fragile peace in the region and prevent Tripoli from being completely overwhelmed.\n\n*Legacy and Death* Raymond III died in 1194. His death marked a turning point in the history of the County of Tripoli.  His successor Raymond IV faced increasing pressure from Saladin and ultimately Tripoli fell to the Ayyubids in 1183.  Despite the eventual loss of the county Raymond III is remembered as a shrewd and resilient ruler who defended Tripoli’s independence for nearly forty years. He left behind a legacy of strong defenses and a well-established administrative system. His reign represents a high point of power and stability for the County of Tripoli during the Crusades. He is viewed as one of the most effective and enduring Counts of Tripoli.\n\n\n\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.0021344278939068317}, {"token": "▁III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -2.9802276912960224e-06}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁ruled", "logprob": -0.23426154255867004}, {"token": "▁from", "logprob": -1.0447988510131836}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -2.95634672511369e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -1.7762025890988298e-05}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -2.50339189733495e-06}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -0.07894306629896164}, {"token": "▁to", "logprob": -0.11286505311727524}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -0.00016544880054425448}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -1.5735502529423684e-05}, {"token": "9", "logprob": -1.4305012882687151e-05}, {"token": "4", "logprob": -0.16472972929477692}, {"token": "▁a", "logprob": -0.01891246624290943}, {"token": "▁significant", "logprob": -0.012677201069891453}, {"token": "▁figure", "logprob": -0.